From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 19:17:07 -0400
Subject: ui: give the window Arc's proportions

Everything that makes the window read as Arc rather than Chromium and is
pure geometry: the rounded content card floating on a mat with all four
corners, the 44 DIP tab rows with 18 DIP favicons drawn crisp from their own
size (no tiling), the 33 DIP toolbar with the URL pill centred and capped at
the reference's width, the bare host shown in the steady state, and the
toolbar icon size. Every metric is a field-trial parameter on
SteddingArcStyleWindow so a pixel match is look-change-look without a rebuild;
docs/UI-SPEC.md carries the measured targets and docs/HANDOFF.md the parameter
names.

Two things this records rather than changes: the toolbar's floor is the macOS
caption buttons, not the exclusion or the icon size, and favicons tiled
because DrawImageInt repeats a 16 px bitmap in an 18 px slot.

The sidebar's furniture follows the reference too: essentials are a grid of
cards, two to a row, and a lone card stays card-sized; Chromium's tab-group and
tab-search combo and the hairline under the top row are hidden; the toolbar
shows no profile avatar.

All four content corners are rounded: the layout merges its radius per corner
with whatever upstream set that pass, because glass mode sets a lower-left
radius every layout and "set ours if upstream set none" never fired, which
left three corners square until a pixel probe said so.

Why: the square edge-to-edge contents, the fat toolbar, the full-width
omnibox and the packed tab rows were the four things that most made the
window read as Chromium; the reference is measured, not eyeballed.
Removable when: upstream's glass frame ships enabled by default and rounds the
contents on all platforms, and the vertical tab strip's and toolbar's metrics
are themable without a patch. Then the parameters become theme values.
